<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="activity-21" top="0.5in" left="0.5in" right="0.5in" bottom="0.5in">
  <title>Lecture Worksheet - Intro to Relativity</title>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Would observers on the North pole agree with those on the South pole 
        as to definitions of <q>up</q> and <q>down</q>? What definitions of 
        the terms could they agree on?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
   <p> 
        The relative velocity <m>~{\vec{V}_{AB}}</m> between two particles A and B 
        is the rate of change of the displacement vector <m>~{\vec{R}_{AB}(t)}</m>
        that connects the two particles at any moment in time. Show that this is 
        invariant under a Galilean transformation (hint: start from a particular 
        reference frame and find an expression for <m>\vec{V}_{AB}</m>
        in terms of measurements in that particular frame).
    </p>
  </exercise>
  <exercise>
    <p>
        In an inelastic collision, the amount of thermal energy 
        (internal mechanical energy) developed is independent of the 
        inertial reference frame of the observer. Explain why, in words.
    </p>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <p>
    Imagine that the universe has some type of speed limit, such that
    <alert>
        no information may be transmitted above that speed.
    </alert>
    Explain why that speed, whatever it is, must be the same for all inertial
    observers. More specifically, explain why a signal that propagates at that
    speed (call it <m>u</m>) must be measured to propagate at the
    <alert>
        same speed
    </alert>
    by every other inertial observer.
    </p>

    <figure>
        <caption>
            Measurements of the speed of electrons as a function of their 
            kinetic energy.
            At high energies, Newtonian physics fails completely (taken from
            <q>Special Relativity</q> by A.P. French)
        </caption>
        <image source="images\activities\lec21\french-graph.png"/>
    </figure>

    <figure>
        <caption>
            Table showing the measurements above quantitatively (<q>flight 
            time</q> is the time it takes for the electrons to travel 8.4m).
            See the film <q>The Ultimate Speed</q>, it's free on YouTube!
        </caption>
        <image width="70%" source="images\activities\lec21\french-table.png"/>
    </figure>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>
